t's it's a bit of a self-selecting  group but the people who used it were  the ones who liked it so  i can't say for sure  no i i think that's it's it's something  to consider i also think it's you know  back to what both what all of the  panelists have said is like also about  how you build the community of of use  around like what is the purpose of it  right like if you're really setting it  up in an authentic way much like we've  heard from the panelists where it's it  is part of the class it's sort of  integrated and and being utilized  sort of in these purposeful ways i i  feel like that might feel a lot more  authentic and students are you know and  course teams are much more likely to  want to engage at that point than if it  sort of  feels just like this is something it's  busy work i'm being forced to post these  things not because we're going to use  them or not because we're going to  engage with them but simply so that you  can check a box that i engaged right  like i think those have very different  feels for students and so the more  authentic you can  uh  the more ways you can foster authentic  engagement the better the the sort of  outcomes are likely to be  um  all right uh i noticed one more  oh a couple of things so you know  jason asked the question um  do you feel a slack increased engagement  um compared to maybe more or other  methods just i guess that's maybe a  question for you i know it's sort of  it's a  it's not the perfect one to one because  it it happened sort of during remote  times but but you know i think you  mentioned that you felt like it had a  pretty positive impact and if you maybe  follow that up a little bit  yeah i think um  yeah i haven't tried using slack during  a sort of quasi-quasi-post-pandemic  world so i can't say  um  i mean maybe i'll try i'm teaching in  the same course actually this spring so  maybe i'll try to use it then and see  how it goes um  but i would say that um  you know my experience with  uh with with teaching this big class in  fall 2020 was that the without slack  students would have basically had piazza  um  lectures were totally  hopeless as far as students interacting  with each other even when i tried to  make small groups happen  uh during lectures um people would just  go into their into their small group  room and sit quietly with their screens  off on mute  it wasn't particular i mean it's just  hard to make um  engagement happen and then the way that  students would interact with each other  is they'd either they'd just talk to  their friends that they had in the class  or to friends they had who had taken the  class previously or whatever so it was  very you know there was almost no  interaction between students um  outside of slack and i think slack was  very useful in um  in getting students to talk to each  other who maybe wouldn't otherwise have  talked to each other so i i think it was  um  versus doing nothing in the in the  remote setting i think it was very  positive whether or not in a  in a situation where there is in-person  classes whether it will have any impact  i yeah it's hard to say i'm not sure  and that and i think uh um  it's a little unfortunate that stefan  had to step out to go teach because i  think his experience this term sort of  reflects a little bit of that that move  towards uh you know utilizing it to  bring people back into the classroom  like the way he sort of set that up to  to be the the pre-class activity that  leverages and is the is the entry point  into the communication in the classroom  really seems to strengthen that  relationship so that oh if i know if if  he's calling out and referencing people  in there and i've got really specific  questions that maybe if i post that it's  going to get addressed or i'm going to  get to engage with other people who have  different ideas you know it starts to  again foster that real authentic  engagement more so than sort of  just i have to post because that's what  the 